<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Zboriv</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1649">1649</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Zboriv was concluded in August 1649 between the Polish king John II Casimir and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>, after a Polish army had been besieged at Zbarazh and the king's relief force was attacked at Zboriv. It recognised Cossack self-rule in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, Bratslav and Chernihiv and raised the Cossack register to 40,000 men. Neither side was satisfied, and war resumed in 1651.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zboriv.jpg" alt="Treaty of Zboriv" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitaliyf261 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_Zaporizhian_Host.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 18 August 1649 after the Battle of Zboriv, where Crown forces of about 35,000 under King John II Casimir clashed with about 50,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-tatars">Crimean Tatars</a> led by Hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky and Khan İslâm III Giray of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. It consisted of two separate agreements, one between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and one between Crimea and the Commonwealth. Ivan Vyhovsky signed beside Khmelnytsky, while Adam Kysil, Jerzy Ossoliński, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-radziwill">Janusz Radziwiłł</a> and Władysław Dominik Zasławski represented the Polish side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>All freedoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a> were kept, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> was to draw up the register himself, to be completed by the feast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intercession-of-the-theotokos">Intercession of the Theotokos</a>. The territory ran on the Right Bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> through Dymer, Korostyshiv, Vinnytsia and Bratslav to Yampil on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>, and on the Left Bank through Oster, Chernihiv, Nizhyn and Romny to the Muscovite border. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chyhyryn">Chyhyryn</a> was made the Cossack Host's city forever and given to Khmelnytsky.</p><p>Past acts in the rising were to be forgotten, and the nobles who had joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic alike, were pardoned. Crown troops, Uniates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were barred from the three voivodeships, offices there were reserved for Orthodox nobles, and the Orthodox Church gained privileges. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> fathers were not to be established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> or the other Ukrainian cities, and a large sum was to be paid to the Crimean Khanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the Commonwealth ratified the treaty on 22 November 1649, approving the declaration made at Zboriv with the consent of all the Estates. Hostilities resumed when Catholic bishops refused to accept its provisions, notably the admission of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, Sylvestr Kosiv, to the Senate. In the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> the treaty is seen as having turned former rebels against the Commonwealth into citizens of a new political community.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Zboriv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Zboriv</b> of 18 August 1649 raised the Registered Cossacks to 40,000 and barred Crown Forces from the Kyiv, Bratslav and Chernihiv voivodeships.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
